  <h1>Signs You Need a Power Flush, Not Just a Bleed</h1>
  <p>A cold radiator doesn't always mean the same problem, and it definitely doesn't always mean you need the most expensive fix. Here's how to tell the difference before you call anyone.</p>

  <h2>Try this first: bleeding</h2>
  <p>If a radiator is cold at the <strong>top</strong> but warm at the bottom, that's almost always trapped air — a 30-second job with a radiator key. This is the cheapest, simplest fix and worth trying before anything else.</p>

  <h2>Signs it's actually sludge, not air</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Radiator is cold at the <strong>bottom</strong>, or cold in patches, rather than just at the top</li>
    <li>You've bled it and it's still not heating evenly</li>
    <li>The water that comes out when bleeding is dark, black, or gritty rather than clear</li>
    <li>Your boiler makes a banging or kettling noise, especially on start-up</li>
    <li>Some rooms heat up fine while others barely warm at all</li>
    <li>Your heating bills have crept up without an obvious reason</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These point to sludge and debris built up inside the system over years — bleeding won't fix this, because the problem isn't air, it's solid gunk sitting in the pipework and radiators.</p>

  <h2>What a power flush actually does</h2>
  <p>A specialist pump and chemical cleaner flush the whole system through, radiator by radiator, clearing out the built-up sludge rather than just moving it around. It restores even heating and takes the strain off your boiler — worth doing before a new boiler install too, so the new unit isn't immediately fighting old sludge.</p>

  <h2>What we'll actually tell you</h2>
  <p>If a bleed or a simple balance will fix it, that's what we'll recommend — we won't sell you a flush you don't need. <a href="/power-flushing/">See our Power Flushing page →</a> or get in touch and describe what's happening; often we can tell which one you need before we even visit.</p>
